<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of Thailand</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gulf of Siam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, formerly the Gulf of Siam, is an inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> bordered by Thailand, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and Malaysia. The Chao Phraya river flows into its northern end, and its sheltered waters gave the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> access to the trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-thailand.jpg" alt="Gulf of Thailand" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gulf_of_Thailand.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
